  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Drying trend this afternoon into Friday with building high\n   pressure. \n\n-  Showers and thunderstorms return this weekend, focusing along\n   the divide, Lemhi County, and southwest Montana. \n\n-  Monitoring trend for widespread precipitation by Wednesday and  \n   Thursday next week with closed low. \n\nRadar images this morning shows a line of showers moving across \nthe Montana-Idaho border towards the divide. An upper level \ntrough passage this morning is driving shower development, with \nhigh-resolution models in strong agreement for activity to pass \neast of the divide by late morning. The highest precipitation \namounts have been found in northwest Montana, with 0.45 inches \nnear Whitefish Lake, 0.34 inches in Libby, and 0.30 inches in \nEureka. Drier air in the low levels has limited precipitation \nsouth of the I-90 corridor. \n\nDry air will move into the Northern Rockies as the trough exits\neastward. High pressure will also begin to build into the area,\nwith skies clearing by afternoon into the evening. Temperatures\nwill trend higher on Friday as southwest flow deepens ahead of the\nnext system entering into the Pacific Northwest. Winds will\nincrease areawide, as a mid-upper level jet moves overhead. Valley\ngusts will range from 20-35 mph, with higher peaks reaching 30-45\nmph. This will bring elevated fire weather conditions across Lemhi\nCounty into southwest Montana. \n\nModel guidance has come into better agreement with a upper level\ntrough and surface cold front tracking across the Northern Rockies\nthis weekend. The trough will split across our area, limiting\nshower coverage and precipitation amounts. Temperatures will trend\nlower on Saturday across Northwest Montana and north-central Idaho\nas the front passes. The front will track southeastward across\nwest-central Montana through southwest Montana by Sunday. Overall,\nthis will be a dry frontal passage, given the splitting nature of\nthe parent trough. Nonetheless, this trough will provide enough\nmoisture and instability for showers and thunderstorms, with\nactivity focused along the divide, Lemhi County, and southwest\nMontana. \n\nEnsemble model guidance has been hinting for another closed low to\nmove into the western US by Wednesday into Thursday next week. In\nfact, recent model runs have been increasing the probabilities for\nwidespread precipitation. The latest NBM has a 60-70% chance for\n0.50 inches or more of precipitation, focused along and south of\nI-90 and along the divide. Overall consenus is beginning to\nincrease for this closed low solution and we will monitor this\nclosely, given the potential for these systems to bring\nsignificant precipitation. The middle of the road forecast \nsuggests a favorable pattern for widespread showers and \nthunderstorm activity Monday into Tuesday ahead of the low.",
